gned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 2-(-2)\\cdot 2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186096645 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364049 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186096645 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364052 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186096645 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364418 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186096674 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362824 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186096674 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364058 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186096678 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363596 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186096678 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364363 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186096679 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364370 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186096756 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362462 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186105663 1071 "Let $X$ be the number obtained by a spinner with four sections labeled $1, 2, 3, 4$. The probability mass function $f(x)$ of $X$ is shown in the table below. What is $\\textrm{Var}[X]$?\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{3}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186105663 1071 "Let $X$ be the number obtained by a spinner with four sections labeled $1, 2, 3, 4.$ The probability mass function $f(x)$ of $X$ is shown in the table below. What is $\\textrm{Var}[X]?$\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{3}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186105663 1074 "The variance of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} (x - \\textrm E[X])^2 \\cdot f(x)\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $\\textrm E[X]$. Summing up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X] &= 1 \\cdot f(1) + 2 \\cdot f(2) + 3 \\cdot f(3) + 4 \\cdot f(4) \\\\\n&= 1(\\frac{1}{6}) + 2(\\frac{1}{4}) + 3(\\frac{1}{3}) + 4(\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{8}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X]=\\dfrac{8}{3}\n$$\n\ninto the variance formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} \\left(x - \\dfrac{8}{3} \\right)^2 \\cdot f(x)\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the variance as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[X] &= (1 - \\frac{8}{3})^{2} \\cdot f(1) + (2 - \\frac{8}{3})^{2} \\cdot f(2) + (3 - \\frac{8}{3})^{2} \\cdot f(3) + (4 - \\frac{8}{3})^{2} \\cdot f(4) \\\\\n&= \\frac{25}{9} \\cdot f(1) + \\frac{4}{9} \\cdot f(2) + \\frac{1}{9} \\cdot f(3) + \\frac{16}{9} \\cdot f(4) \\\\\n&= \\frac{25}{9}(\\frac{1}{6}) + \\frac{4}{9} \\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}) + \\frac{1}{9} \\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}) + \\frac{16}{9}(\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{19}{18}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186105663 1074 "The variance of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} (x - \\textrm E[X])^2 \\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $\\textrm E[X].$ Summing up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3)+4\\cdot f(4) \\\\  & =1(\\frac{1}{6})+2(\\frac{1}{4})+3(\\frac{1}{3})+4(\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\textrm E[X]=\\dfrac{8}{3}$ into the variance formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} \\left(x - \\dfrac{8}{3} \\right)^2 \\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the variance as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & ={(1-\\frac{8}{3})}^{2}\\cdot f(1)+{(2-\\frac{8}{3})}^{2}\\cdot f(2)+{(3-\\frac{8}{3})}^{2}\\cdot f(3)+{(4-\\frac{8}{3})}^{2}\\cdot f(4) \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{9}\\cdot f(1)+\\frac{4}{9}\\cdot f(2)+\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot f(3)+\\frac{16}{9}\\cdot f(4) \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{9}(\\frac{1}{6})+\\frac{4}{9}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4})+\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3})+\\frac{16}{9}(\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{18}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186105664 1071 "A fair die with sides labeled with numbers $1$ through $6$ is thrown once. If $X$ is the outcome of the die, what is $\\textrm{SD}[X]$?" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186105664 1071 "A fair die with sides labeled with numbers $1$ through $6$ is thrown once. If $X$ is the outcome of the die, what is $\\textrm{SD}[X]?$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186105664 1074 "The standard deviation of a random variable $X$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SD}[X] = \\sqrt{ \\textrm{Var}[X] },\n$$\n\nwhere the variance can be computed using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm E[X^2] - \\textrm E[X]^2\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute the probability mass function $f(x).$ Here, there are $6$ possible outcomes ($1,2,3,4,5,6$) and each outcome has the same probability, so the probability mass function is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ |\n\nNext, we compute $\\textrm E[X].$ Summing up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3)+4\\cdot f(4)+5\\cdot f(5)+6\\cdot f(6) \\\\  & =1(\\frac{1}{6})+2(\\frac{1}{6})+3(\\frac{1}{6})+4(\\frac{1}{6})+5(\\frac{1}{6})+6(\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $\\textrm E[X^2].$ Summing up the products of each value of $X^2$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={1}^{2}\\cdot f(1)+{2}^{2}\\cdot f(2)+{3}^{2}\\cdot f(3)+{4}^{2}\\cdot f(4)+{5}^{2}\\cdot f(5)+{6}^{2}\\cdot f(6) \\\\  & =1(\\frac{1}{6})+4(\\frac{1}{6})+9(\\frac{1}{6})+16(\\frac{1}{6})+25(\\frac{1}{6})+36(\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{91}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{91}{6}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{91}{6}-\\frac{49}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{35}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{SD}[X] & =\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[X]} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{35}{12}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186105664 1074 "The standard deviation of a random variable $X$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SD}[X] = \\sqrt{\\textrm{Var}[X]}\n$$\n\nwhere the variance can be computed using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm E[X^2] - \\textrm E[X]^2\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute the probability mass function $f(x)$. Here, there are $6$ possible outcomes ($1,2,3,4,5,6$) and each outcome has the same probability, so the probability mass function is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ |\n\nNext, we compute $\\textrm E[X]$. Summing up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X] &= 1 \\cdot f(1) + 2 \\cdot f(2) + 3 \\cdot f(3) + 4 \\cdot f(4) + 5 \\cdot f(5) + 6 \\cdot f(6) \\\\\n&= 1(\\frac{1}{6}) + 2(\\frac{1}{6}) + 3(\\frac{1}{6}) + 4(\\frac{1}{6}) + 5(\\frac{1}{6}) + 6(\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $\\textrm E[X^2]$. Summing up the products of each value of $X^2$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X^{2}] &= 1^{2} \\cdot f(1) + 2^{2} \\cdot f(2) + 3^{2} \\cdot f(3) + 4^{2} \\cdot f(4) + 5^{2} \\cdot f(5) + 6^{2} \\cdot f(6) \\\\\n&= 1(\\frac{1}{6}) + 4(\\frac{1}{6}) + 9(\\frac{1}{6}) + 16(\\frac{1}{6}) + 25(\\frac{1}{6}) + 36(\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{91}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[X] &= E[X^{2}] - E[X]^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{91}{6} - (\\frac{7}{2})^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{91}{6} - \\frac{49}{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{35}{12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nSD[X] &= \\sqrt{Var[X]} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{35}{12}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186105668 1071 "A fair die with sides labeled with numbers $1$ through $6$ is thrown once. If $X$ is the outcome of the die, what is the variance of $X$?" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186105668 1071 "A fair die with sides labeled with numbers $1$ through $6$ is thrown once. If $X$ is the outcome of the die, what is the variance of $X?$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186105668 1074 "We can compute the variance of a discrete random variable $X$ using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm E[X^2] - \\textrm E[X]^2\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute the probability mass function $f(x)$. Here, there are $6$ possible outcomes ($1,2,3,4,5,6$) and each outcome has the same probability, so the probability mass function is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ | $\