^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,2⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)}^{2}+{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)}^{2}+{(2)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡2t+4{cos}^{2}⁡2t+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({sin}^{2}⁡2t+{cos}^{2}⁡2t)+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+4} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are told that\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)=8(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,j+\\,k),\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖ & =8\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)^{2}+(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{2}+(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡2t+{cos}^{2}⁡2t+1} \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{{(2\\sqrt{2})}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{16\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186120250 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117629 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186120250 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363763 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186120250 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186120250 1071 "Consider the straight line with the parametric equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-1+2t \\\\ y=2+4t \\\\ z=-5+3t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Find the Cartesian equation of this line." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186120250 1071 "Consider the straight line with the parametric equations\n${x =-1 + 2t; y = 2 + 4t; z =-5 + 3t$\nfor $t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)$. Find the Cartesian equation of this line." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186120250 1074 "First, let's find the corresponding vector equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1+2t \\\\ 2+4t \\\\ -5+3t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2t \\\\ 4t \\\\ 3t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nThis implies that the given line passes through the point $P(-1,2,-5)$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨2,4,3⟩.$ So, its Cartesian (canonical) equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-(-1)}{2}=\\frac{y-2}{4}=\\frac{z-(-5)}{3}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we reach\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+1}{2}=\\frac{y-2}{4}=\\frac{z+5}{3}.\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186120503 1068 #edb/ref 17592186119102 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186120503 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364283 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186120503 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186120503 1071 "Given the curve $r(t) = ⟨t^{2} + 1, t^{2} - 1, 2t⟩$, use the cross product curvature formula to find the curvature as a function of $t$." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186120503 1071 "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2}+1,\\,{t}^{2}-1,\\,2t⟩$, use the cross product curvature formula to find the curvature as a function of $t.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186120503 1074 "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)⟩=⟨2t,\\,2t,\\,2⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+(2t)^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+4{t}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4(2{t}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the second derivative ${r}^{″}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2)⟩=⟨2,\\,2,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the cross-product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =⟨2t,2t,2⟩\\times ⟨2,2,0⟩ \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2t & 2t & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2t & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}2t & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}2t & 2t \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =-4i+4j+(4t-4t)k \\\\  & =-4\\,i+4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the magnitude of the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{32} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}=\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{{[2\\sqrt{2{t}^{2}+1}]}^{3}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2(2{t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186120512 1068 #edb/ref 17592186119156 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186120512 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364330 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186120512 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186120512 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186120512 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n$r = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + t\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}, t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186120512 1074 "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-(-1)}{0}=\\frac{y-1}{2}=\\frac{z-0}{-1}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+1}{0}=\\frac{y-1}{2}=-z,\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because $\\frac{x+1}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $x$ component always maintains the constant value of $x=-1:$\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1+2t \\\\ -t\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\frac{x+1}{0}$ part and instead write $x=-1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{y-1}{2}=-z,\\,x=-1\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186120513 1068 #edb/ref 17592186119162 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186120513 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364336 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186120513 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186120513 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186120513 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n$r = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + t\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}, t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186120513 1074 "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{5}=\\frac{y-7}{0}=\\frac{z-1}{-1}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{5}=\\frac{y-7}{0}=1-z,\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because $\\frac{y-7}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $y$ component always maintains the constant value of $y=7:$\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}2+5t \\\\ 7 \\\\ 1-t\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\