92186124819 1074 "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local maximum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\le f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the highest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch neighborhoods exist for the points $B$ and $D.$ Hence $B$ and $D$ are local maxima.\n\n![](images/44/4464c66c00bc020668bef5e346caee352228403068be1334fc4997439140ae7f.png)" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124886 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121886 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186124886 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364137 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124886 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124886 1071 "The row echelon form matrix $B$ is given below. What are its **non**-pivot columns?\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 3 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124886 1071 "The row echelon form matrix $B$ is given below. What are its **non**-pivot columns?\n$B = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 & 3 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186124886 1074 "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $B$ has $4$ pivots in the $1$st, $2$nd, $4$th, and $5$th columns:\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 3 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only non-pivot column is the $3$rd column." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186125045 1068 #edb/ref 17592186122840 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186125045 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364157 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186125045 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186125045 1071 "Given that $f(x, y) = 2xy + y^{2}$, find $\\frac{∂f}{∂θ}$, where $(x, y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r, θ)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186125045 1071 "Given that $f(x,y)=2xy+{y}^{2},$ find $\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta },$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186125045 1074 "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂f}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂f}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2x+2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ) \\\\  & =2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186128850 1071 "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the cylinder $x^2 + y^2 = 4$ for $y \\geq 0$ between the surfaces $z =0$ and $z = 10 - x$. Express the following triple integral in cylindrical polar coordinates.\n$\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R y \\:\\textrm{d}V$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186128850 1071 "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the cylinder $x^2 + y^2 = 4$ for $y \\geq 0$ between the surfaces $z =0$ and $z = 10 - x.$ Express the following triple integral in cylindrical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R y \\:\\textrm{d}V\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186128850 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\, \\mathrm{d}V = \n\\iiint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\, f(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta, z) \\:  r \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\, \\mathrm{d}r \\, \\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nIf $(r, \\theta, z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad\ny = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad \nz = z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/9a/9ad0d507a5440cfce9595e9054457fd733ae412d9a57b0a85238b4773ff0a0c7.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=10-x = 10 - r\\cos\\theta.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta,z) \\, : \\, 0 \\leq r \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\pi, \\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 10-r\\cos\\theta \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}y\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{10-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186128850 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\, \\mathrm{d}V = \\iiint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\, f(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta, z) \\: r \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\, \\mathrm{d}r \\, \\mathrm{d}\\theta\n$$\n\nIf $(r, \\theta, z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad z = z\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/9a/9ad0d507a5440cfce9595e9054457fd733ae412d9a57b0a85238b4773ff0a0c7.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0$, and\n- bounded above by $z=10-x = 10 - r\\cos\\theta$.\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{(r,\\theta,z) \\,: \\, 0 \\leq r \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\pi, \\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 10-r\\cos\\theta \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∭_(R)ydV &= ∭_(Δ)(r\\sin θ) \\cdot rdzdrdθ \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{π}\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{10 - r\\cos θ}r^{2}\\sin θdzdrdθ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186128851 1071 "If the solid region $R$ is given by\n$R = \\left\\{(x,y,z) \\,: \\, (x-1)^2 + y^2 \\leq 1, \\: -2 \\leq z \\leq 4-y \\right\\}$,\nthen express the triple integral\n$\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} \\: \\textrm{d}V$\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186128851 1071 "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR = \\left\\{ (x,y,z) \\, : \\, (x-1)^2 + y^2 \\leq 1, \\: -2 \\leq z \\leq 4-y \\right\\},\n$$\n\nthen express the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} \\: \\textrm{d}V\n$$\n\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186128851 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\, \\mathrm{d}V = \n\\iiint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\, f(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta, z) \\:  r \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\, \\mathrm{d}r \\, \\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nIf $(r, \\theta, z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad\ny = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad \nz = z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/a5/a551e379767395a2422f690a66fc38defab64cff03c752c0c9a5a0748561d2f7.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $1$ whose axis passes through the point $(1,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z = -2,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z = 4 - y = 4 - r\\sin{\\theta}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write the equation $(x-1)^2 + y^2 = 1$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1)}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r = 2\\cos{\\theta}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta,z) \\, : \\, 0 \\leq r \\leq 2\\cos{\\theta}, \\: -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\: -2 \\leq z \\leq 4-r\\sin{\\theta} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-2}^{4-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186128851 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\, \\mathrm{d}V = \\iiint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\, f(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta, z) \\: r \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\, \\mathrm{d}r \\, \\mathrm{d}\\theta\n$$\n\nIf $(r, \\theta, z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad z = z\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/a5/a551e379767395a2422f690a66fc38defab64cff03c752c0c9a5a0748561d2f7.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $1$ whose axis passes through the point $(1,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z = -2$, and\n- bounded above by $z = 4 - y = 4 - r\\sin{\\theta}$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's write the equation\n\n$$\n(x-1)^2 + y^2 = 1\n$$\n\nin polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x - 1)^{2} + y^{2} &= 1 \\\\\n(r\\cos θ - 1)^{2} + (r\\sin θ)^{2} &= 1 \\\\\nr^{2}\\cos^{2} θ - 2r\\cos θ + 1 + r^{2}\\sin^{2} θ &= 1 \\\\\nr^{2}(\\cos^{2} θ + \\sin^{2} θ) - 2r\\cos θ &= 0 \\\\\nr^{2} - 2r\\cos θ &= 0 \\\\\nr(r - 2\\cos θ) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have\n\n$$\nr = 2\\cos{\\theta}\n$$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{(r,\\theta,z) \\,: \\, 0 \\leq r \\leq 2\\cos{\\theta}, \\: -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\: -2 \\leq z \\leq 4-r\\sin{\\theta} \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∭_(R)\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}dV &= ∭_(Δ)\\sqrt{(r\\cos θ)^{2} + (r\\sin θ)^{2}}rdzdrdθ \\\\\n&= ∭_(Δ)\\sqrt{r^{2}(\\cos^{2} θ + \\sin^{2} θ)}rdzdrdθ \\\\\n&= \\int_{- π/2}^{π/2}\\int_{0}^{2\\cos θ}\\int_{-2}^{4 - r\\sin θ}r^{2}dzdrdθ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186128862 1071 "Evaluate the triple integral\n$\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R x+2y \\:\\textrm{d}V$\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder $x^2+y^2=5$ for $x\\geq 0$, $y\\geq 0$ and between the surfaces $z=y$ and $z=y+3$." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186128862 1071 "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R x+2y \\:\\textrm{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder $x^2+y^2=5$ for $x\\geq 0,$ $y\\geq 0$ and between the surfaces $z=y$ and $z=y+3.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186128862 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\, \\mathrm{d}V = \n\\iiint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\, f(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta, z) \\:  r \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\, \\mathrm{d}r \\, \\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nIf $(r, \\theta, z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad\ny = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad \nz = z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/b7/b7b37ffe8e97ed4a661ee98750894641c6ed3354864dd5b54d2ed076e4fdb69b.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $\\sqrt{5}$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=y=r\\sin\\theta,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=y+3=r\\sin\\theta + 3.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta,z) \\, : \\, 0 \\leq r \\leq \\sqrt{5}, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\lt \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\: r\\sin\\theta \\leq z \\leq r\\sin\\theta + 3\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x+2y\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }^{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )[z]_{z=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }^{z=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}3{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[{r}^{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )]_{r=0}^{r=\\sqrt{5}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{(\\sqrt{5})}^{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}5\\sqrt{5}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{5}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =\\pi /2} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{5}(1-0)-10\\sqrt{5}(0-1) \\\\  & =15\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186128862 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\, \\mathrm{d}V = \\iiin