n\n \nwhere $\\mathbf F$ is the *resultant* force, $m$ is the mass, and $\\mathbf a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{e}^{t},\\,3{t}^{2}-2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resultant force $\\mathbf F$ at time $t$ equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =5\\cdot ⟨3{e}^{t},\\,3{t}^{2}-2t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨15{e}^{t},\\,15{t}^{2}-10t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $t = 2\\,\\textrm s,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2) & =⟨15{e}^{2},\\,15({2}^{2})-10(2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨15{e}^{2},\\,40⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the magnitude of $\\mathbf F(2)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|F(2)| & =\\sqrt{{(15{e}^{2})}^{2}+{(40)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{225{e}^{4}+1\\,600} \\\\  & ≈118\\,\\text{N}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest Newton." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186171915 1071 "The displacement $\\mathbf r$, in meters, of a particle $P$ of mass $3\\,\\textrm{kg}$ is given by\n$\\mathbf r = \\langle 3\\cos{t}-\\sin{t},\\: 2\\cos{t}\\rangle$,\nwhere $t\\gt 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the resultant force acting on the particle when $t=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}\\,\\textrm s$." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186171915 1071 "The displacement $\\mathbf r,$ in meters, of a particle $P$ of mass $3\\,\\textrm{kg}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r =  \\langle 3\\cos{t}-\\sin{t},\\: 2\\cos{t}\\rangle,\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\gt 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the resultant force acting on the particle when $t=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}\\,\\textrm s.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186171915 1074 "Newton’s second law states that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F = m \\mathbf a\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf F$ is the *resultant* force, $m$ is the mass, and $\\mathbf a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo compute the acceleration vector, we first differentiate $\\mathbf r$ to get the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv &= \\frac{dr}{dt} \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(3\\cos t - \\sin t), \\frac{d}{dt}(2\\cos t)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 3\\sin t - \\cos t,-2\\sin t⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate the velocity vector to get the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na &= \\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(-3\\sin t - \\cos t), \\frac{d}{dt}(-2\\sin t)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 3\\cos t + \\sin t,-2\\cos t⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resultant force $\\mathbf F$ at time $t$ equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF &= ma \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot ⟨ - 3\\cos t + \\sin t,-2\\cos t⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨3\\sin t - 9\\cos t,-6\\cos t⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when\n\n$$\nt = \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}\\,\\textrm s\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(\\frac{3π}{4}) &= ⟨3\\sin (\\frac{3π}{4}) - 9\\cos (\\frac{3π}{4}),-6\\cos (\\frac{3π}{4})⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2} + \\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\frac{6\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨6\\sqrt{2}, 3\\sqrt{2}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the resultant force acting on $P$ when\n\n$$\nt=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}\\,\\textrm s\n$$\n\nequals\n\n$$\n\\left\\langle 6\\sqrt{2},\\: 3\\sqrt{2}\\right\\rangle\\,\\textrm N\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186171915 1074 "Newton’s second law states that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F = m \\mathbf a,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $\\mathbf F$ is the *resultant* force, $m$ is the mass, and $\\mathbf a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo compute the acceleration vector, we first differentiate $\\mathbf r$ to get the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate the velocity vector to get the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resultant force $\\mathbf F$ at time $t$ equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =3\\cdot ⟨-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $t = \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}\\,\\textrm s,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) & =⟨3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})-9\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}),\\,-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,\\frac{6\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6\\sqrt{2},\\,3\\sqrt{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the resultant force acting on $P$ when $t=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}\\,\\textrm s$ equals $\\left\\langle 6\\sqrt{2},\\: 3\\sqrt{2}\\right\\rangle\\,\\textrm N.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186171916 1071 "A particle $P$ of mass $6\\,\\textrm{kg}$ is subject to the external forces $\\mathbf F_1$ and $\\mathbf F_2$, given by\n$\\mathbf F_1 = \\left\\langle \\dfrac2{t+1}+12t,\\: \\dfrac3{\\sqrt t} \\right\\rangle, \\qquad \\mathbf F_2 = \\left\\langle \\dfrac 4{t+1},\\: 6\\right\\rangle$,\nwhere $\\mathbf F_1$ and $\\mathbf F_2$ are measured in Newtons and $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the velocity of $P$ equals $\\langle -\\ln 3,\\:-2\\rangle\\,\\textrm{m/s}$ when $t=0\\,\\textrm s$, calculate the speed of $P$ when $t=2\\,\\textrm s$." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186171916 1071 "A particle $P$ of mass $6\\,\\textrm{kg}$ is subject to the external forces $\\mathbf F_1$ and $\\mathbf F_2,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F_1 = \\left\\langle \\dfrac2{t+1}+12t,\\: \\dfrac3{\\sqrt t} \\right\\rangle, \\qquad  \\mathbf F_2 = \\left\\langle \\dfrac 4{t+1},\\: 6\\right\\rangle,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $\\mathbf F_1$ and $\\mathbf F_2$ are measured in Newtons and $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the velocity of $P$ equals $\\langle -\\ln 3,\\:-2\\rangle\\,\\textrm{m/s}$ when $t=0\\,\\textrm s,$ calculate the speed of $P$ when $t=2\\,\\textrm s.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186171916 1074 "Newton's second law states that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F = m \\mathbf a\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf F$ is the *resultant* force, $m$ is the mass, and $\\mathbf a$ is the acceleration.\n\nThe resultant force $\\mathbf F$ acting on $P$ equals the sum of the forces acting on $P$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF &= F_{1} + F_{2} \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{2}{t + 1} + 12t, \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{t}}⟩ + ⟨\\frac{4}{t + 1}, 6⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{6}{t + 1} + 12t, \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{t}} + 6⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy Newton's second law, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na &= \\frac{F}{m} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6} \\cdot ⟨\\frac{6}{t + 1} + 12t, \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{t}} + 6⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{1}{t + 1} + 2t, \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}} + 1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the velocity vector $\\mathbf v$, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv &= \\intadt \\\\\n&= \\int⟨\\frac{1}{t + 1} + 2t, \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}} + 1⟩dt \\\\\n&= ⟨\\int(\\frac{1}{t + 1} + 2t)dt, \\int(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}} + 1)dt⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\ln (t + 1) + t^{2}, \\sqrt{t} + t⟩ + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf C$ is a (vector) constant of integration.\n\nWe can solve for $\\mathbf C$ by substituting\n\n$$\n\\mathbf v = \\langle -\\ln 3,\\:-2\\rangle\\,\\textrm{m/s}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nt=0\\,\\textrm s\n$$\n\ninto the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨ - \\ln 3,-2⟩ &= ⟨\\ln (0 + 1) + 0^{2}, \\sqrt{0} + 0⟩ + C \\\\\n⟨ - \\ln 3,-2⟩ &= ⟨0, 0⟩ + C \\\\\nC &= ⟨ - \\ln 3,-2⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity of $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv &= ⟨\\ln (t + 1) + t^{2}, \\sqrt{t} + t⟩ + ⟨ - \\ln 3,-2⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\ln (t + 1) + t^{2} - \\ln 3, \\sqrt{t} + t - 2⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $t=2$, the velocity equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(2) &= ⟨\\ln (2 + 1) + 2^{2} - \\ln 3, \\sqrt{2} + 2 - 2⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨4, \\sqrt{2}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the speed of $P$ when $t=2$ equals the magnitude of $\\mathbf v(2)$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid v(2) \\mid &= \\sqrt{4^{2} + (\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{16 + 2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{18} \\\\\n&= 3\\sqrt{2}m/s\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186171916 1074 "Newton's second law states that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F = m \\mathbf a,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $\\mathbf F$ is the *resultant* force, $m$ is the mass, and $\\mathbf a$ is the acceleration.\n\nThe resultant force $\\mathbf F$ acting on $P$ equals the sum of the forces acting on $P.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & ={F}_{1}+{F}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{t+1}+12t,\\,\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{t}}⟩+⟨\\frac{4}{t+1},\\,6⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{6}{t+1}+12t,\\,\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{t}}+6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy Newton's second law, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{F}{m} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot ⟨\\frac{6}{t+1}+12t,\\,\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{t}}+6⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{t+1}+2t,\\,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}+1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the velocity vector $\\mathbf v,$ we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\int a\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨\\frac{1}{t+1}+2t,\\,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}+1⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int (\\frac{1}{t+1}+2t)\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int (\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}+1)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)+{t}^{2},\\,\\sqrt{t}+t⟩+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf C$ is a (vector) constant of integration. \n\nWe can solve for $\\mathbf C$ by substituting $\\mathbf v = \\langle -\\ln 3,\\:-2\\rangle\\,\\textrm{m/s}$ and $t=0\\,\\textrm s$ into the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3,\\,-2⟩ & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0+1)+{0}^{2},\\,\\