his into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+({x}_{3})=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get  \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{-1}{1}=-1.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186176252 1068 #edb/ref 17592186173740 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186176252 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362660 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186176252 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186176252 1071 "Which of the following is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ for the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186176252 1071 "Which of the following is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $λ =-4$ for the matrix $A$, given by\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$?" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186176252 1074 "Given a matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda ,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $k$ is a vector $v$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)^{k}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-\\lambda I)^{k-1}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTo find a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-4,$ we need to find a vector $v$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n(A+4I)^{2}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A+4I)v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each condition:\n\n- We start by solving $(A+4I)^{2}v=0.$ First, we have\n\n$$\nA+4I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nComputing its square, we see that this matrix is nilpotent:\n\n$$\n(A+4I)^{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis means that *any* vector in ${R}^{2}$ is a solution to $(A+4I)^{2}v=0.$ Therefore, the solution to $(A+4I)^{2}v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2}\\end{aligned}]={v}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{v}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $(A+4I)v=0.$ We have\n\n$$\nA+4I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,.\n$$\n\nConverting this matrix to reduced row echelon form, we get\n\n$$\nA+4I∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis means that the solution to $(A+4I)v=0$ is ${v}_{1}=0,$ where ${v}_{2}$ is a free variable. Therefore, the solution to $(A+4I)v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\end{aligned}]={v}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing our two solutions, we see that the vector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ satisfies $(A+4I)^{2}v=0$ but does *not* satisfy $(A+4I)v=0.$  \n\nHence, $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ of $A.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186176261 1068 #edb/ref 17592186173794 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186176261 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362729 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186176261 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186176261 1071 "The random variable $X$ has the following probability distribution.\n\n| $x$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ |\n\nCalculate $P({X}^{2}=1).$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186176261 1071 "The random variable $X$ has the following probability distribution.\n\n| $x$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ |\n\nCalculate $P(X^{2} = 1)$." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186176261 1074 "Let's first