ns below.The Jacobian determinant of the system at the equilibrium point $x^{∗} = [0, 0]^{T}$ is given as\n$\\begin{vmatrix}0 & π \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}$ {{field-1}}\n\nAt this point, the system is\n{{field-2}}\nat $x^{∗}$." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186222011 1074 "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\nJ=|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nJ=|\\begin{aligned}2x & 2y \\\\ 2x & -2y\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J(0,0) & =|\\begin{aligned}2(0) & 2(0) \\\\ 2(0) & -2(0)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the Jacobian determinant is zero, our system is $\\text{not almost linear}$ at ${x}^{∗}.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186222014 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220728 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186222014 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220732 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186222014 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363822 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186222014 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363825 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186222014 1