(b)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, the BVP has a unique, trivial solution $y≡0$ if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nIn other words, if the determinant equals zero, then the BVP has *infinitely many solutions*.\n\nNotice that, in our case, we have Neumann boundary conditions. So, setting ${\\alpha }_{i}=0,{\\beta }_{i}=1,a=0,$ and $b=\\pi ,$ the matrix $A$ reduces to\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(0) & {y}_{2}^{′}(0) \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′}(\\pi ) & {y}_{2}^{′}(\\pi )\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+4 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 2\\text{i}.$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\text{ and }{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}^{′}(x)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x,\\,{y}_{2}^{′}(x)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0) & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\pi ) & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\pi )\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =0\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(A)=0$ we conclude that this BVP $\\text{has infinitely many solutions}.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186229741 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228640 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186229741 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228647 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186229741 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363964 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186229741 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363971 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186229741 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186229741 1071 "Use the definition of Laplace transforms to calculate $F(s) = L{f(t)}$, where $f(t)$ is given by\n$f(t) = {e^{-2t},\\begin{vmatrix}t \\le 3 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}t > 3$.$F(s) = L{f(t)} =$ {{field-1}},\nvalid for {{field-2}}." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186229741 1071 "Use the definition of Laplace transforms to calculate $F(s)=L{f(t)},$ where $f(t)$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(t)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2t},\\, & t\\le 3 \\\\ 1\\, & t>3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$\\,F(s)=L{f(t)}=$ {{field-1}},\nvalid for {{field-2}}." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186229741 1074 "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(t)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2t},\\, & t\\le 3 \\\\ 1\\, & t>3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{-2t}\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }1\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{-2t-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }1\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{-(2+s)t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the improper integral diverges if $s\\le 0.$\n\nFor $s>0,$ we calculate our Laplace transform as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{-(2+s)t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-(2+s)}{[{e}^{-(2+s)t}]}_{0}^{3}+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-3(2+s)}}{2+s}+{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{3}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-3(2+s)}}{2+s}+{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{-{e}^{-st}}{s})|}_{3}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-3(2+s)}}{2+s}-\\frac{1}{s}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({e}^{-as}-{e}^{-3s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-3(2+s)}}{2+s}-\\frac{1}{s}\\cdot (0-{e}^{-3s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-3(2+s)}}{2+s}+\\frac{{e}^{-3s}}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvalid for $s>0.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362462 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362463 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362462 1069 "q-10032" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362462 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362462 1071 "![](images/9e/9e19c1cd8bca76f1eec39acfa1b09c0f08b67faea5ddce8325e093923d21d394.png)\n\nFind the area bounded between the curve $y=\\sqrt[3]{x+1},$ the $y$-axis and the lines $y=0$ and $y=1,$ as shown above." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362462 1072 #uuid "a2c8f25f-d60e-58d8-adfc-09591d2ab6e3" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362462 1074 "First, we make $x$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt[3]{x+1} \\\\ {y}^{3} & =x+1 \\\\ x & ={y}^{3}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{3}-1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}|}_{0}^{1}-y{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4}-0)-(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}-1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtained a negative number because the region lies to the left of the $y$-axis. So the area that we want is $|-\\frac{3}{4}|=\\frac{3}{4}.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362463 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362464 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362463 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362465 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362463 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362466 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362463 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362467 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362463 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362468 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362463 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362463 1077 "selection" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362463 1079 #edb/ref 17592186362467 13194139533445 1759218604