45 17592186045585 true] [17592186363306 1074 "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2x)+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2y) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=\\sqrt{3}$ and $x=-\\sqrt{3}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2\\cdot (-\\sqrt{3}))+(\\sqrt{3})\\cdot (2y) & =0. \\\\ -2\\sqrt{3}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2\\sqrt{3}y & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also want to substitute for $y,$ but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $x=-\\sqrt{3}$ into the equation of the circle.\n\nAt the point where $x=-\\sqrt{3},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {(-\\sqrt{3})}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ 3+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ y & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used $y>0$ since we're only interested in the second \nquadrant.\n\nFinally, substituting $y=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363307 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363308 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363307 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363309 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363307 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363310 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363307 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363311 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363307 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363312 13194139533445