4139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363723 1071 "Consider a function $q(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $L{q(t)}=Q(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Suppose we wish to find $L{{e}^{9t}q(t)}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.By definition of the Laplace transform, we can write \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{9t}q(t)} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-1}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}\\,q(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={{field-3}}\\,\\text{for}\\,{{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363723 1072 #uuid "f9cd6f01-d8c7-5536-9f5a-869210d50728" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363723 1074 "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{9t}q(t)} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{9t}\\,q(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-9)t}\\,q(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $Q(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,q(t)\\,\\text{d}t,$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-9)t}\\,q(t)\\,\\text{d}t=Q(s-9),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns-9>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}+9.\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363724 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363725 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363724 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363726 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363724 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363727 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363724 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363724 1077 "field-3" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363724 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363726 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363724 1080 #uuid "cf5bb005-5f3f-5af5-89f2-8ea2fb3c315f" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363725 1081 "Q(s)" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363725 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363725 1084 #uuid "fb8a4d5f-9a6d-54d5-8678-e59f13f43d9f" 131