{2}(x)={e}^{-x}$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363903 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363903 1084 #uuid "a0a1b63c-e53c-507d-9c47-d1c40d02463c" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363904 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363905 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363904 1069 "q-338536" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363904 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363904 1071 "Which of the following systems is a boundary value problem?\n\n- ${y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-y={x}^{2},\\,y(0)=1,\\,y(2)=3$\n- ${y}^{″}-4y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,y(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=2$\n- ${y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+y={e}^{x}$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363904 1072 #uuid "67ebcbc8-9a0b-5eef-bc58-dbb711498ad0" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363904 1074 "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at *different* locations in the domain of $y,$ then this is a **boundary value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$ and $x=2$).\n\n- The second system is not a boundary value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the *same* location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$). This is an **initial value problem.**\n\n- The third system is not a boundary value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363905 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363906 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363905 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363907 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363905 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363908 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363905 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363909 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363905 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363910 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363905 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363905 1077 "selection" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363905 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363908 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363905 1080 #uuid "8caa9690-b35c-5a66-a23b-26780bada7bd" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363906 1081 "II and III only" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363906 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363906 1084 #uuid "dc469ea1-79c9-52d1-81a6-2552c0d7b7a5" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363907 1081 "I and III only" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363907 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363907 1084 #uuid "c9f5c5e9-8572-5a90-8258-c0b86e590584" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363908 1081 "I only" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363908 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363908 1084 #uuid "286b767a-7e4b-5004-96bc-dfa2fa2a10d2" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363909 1081 "I and II only" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363909 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363909 1084 #uuid "1deef986-7713-552b-bba4-ed0a01c44300" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363910 1081 "II only" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363910 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363910 1084 #uuid "fff00a93-c9be-561a-844f-19a7dab63479" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363911 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363912 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363911 1069 "q-338613" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363911 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363911 1071 "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0,\\,y(3)=0,\\,y(5)-2{y}^{′}(5)=0,\\,3\\le x\\le 5\n$$\n\nFind the solution to this boundary value problem in terms of an arbitrary constant $A.$\n\nThe solution is $y=A\\,\\cdot ($\n{{field-1}}$).$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363911 1072 #uuid "ad36d666-c464-5b30-84d6-15e29368cf86" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363911 1074 "To solve the homogeneous boundary value problem, we first find the general solution and then determine the values of the arbitrary constants that fit the boundary conditions.\n\nWe have the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation twice, we get the general solution\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=A.\n$$\n\nApplying the condition $y(3)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n3A+B=0\\,⇒\\,B=-3A.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\ny=Ax-3A=A(x-3).\n$$\n\nApplying the condition $y(5)-2{y}^{′}(5)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n2A-2A=0\n$$\n\nwhich is automatically satisfied for any $A.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the BVP is $y=A\\cdot (x-3),$ where $A$ is arbitrary." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363912 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363913 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363912 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363912 1077 "field-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363912 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363913 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363912 1080 #uuid "11f94d2d-80d2-5776-bfef-d0d09fe9d970" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363913 1081 "x-3" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363913 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363913 1084 #uuid "df1e4981-455d-5f6f-b9c9-724964a712fc" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363914 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363915 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363914 1069 "q-338615" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363914 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363914 1071 "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)={y}^{′}\\,(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=0,\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{2\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to this boundary value problem in terms of an arbitrary constant $A.$\n\nThe solution is $y=A\\,\\cdot $\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363914 1072 #uuid "0f937058-b16d-55cd-860e-1d64cdcf4a86" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363914 1074 "To solve the homogeneous boundary value problem, we first find the general solution and then determine the values of the arbitrary constants that fit the boundary conditions.\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+9 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 3\\text{i}.$ So, the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-3A\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+3B\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nApplying the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n-3A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot 0)+3B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot 0)=0\\,⇒\\,B=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nApplying the condition ${y}^{′}\\,(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n-3A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )=0,\n$$\n\nwhich is automatically satisfied for any $A.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the BVP is $y=A\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),$ where $A$ is arbitrary." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363915 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363916 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363915 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363915 1077 "field-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363915 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363916 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363915 1080 #uuid "a6ac8688-692e-5a8e-bb14-fc3b00c3ce54" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363916 1081 "\\operatorname{cos}(3x)" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363916 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363916 1084 #uuid "957cf1e6-d4c7-52fd-8473-50c0ad02e0f9" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363917 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363918 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363917 1069 "q-338640" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363917 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363917 1071 "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+4{y}^{′}(x)+5y(x)={x}^{2},\\,y(1)=y(6),\\,{y}^{′}(1)={y}^{′}(6),\\,1\\le x\\le 6\n$$\n\nWhich of the following best classifies the boundary conditions for this problem?" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363917 1072 #uuid "ee752651-fd40-5139-b8ea-e4b55393d8d0" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363917 1074 "For a second-order boundary value problem, \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}(x)+Q(x)y(x)=f(x),\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x),Q(x)$ and $f(x)$ are all continuous on $[a,b],$ we have the following boundary condition classifications. \n\n- Dirichlet:\n\n$$\ny(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,y(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Neumann:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Robin (mixed):\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Periodic:\n\n$$\ny(a)=y(b),\\,{y}^{′}(a)={y}^{′}(b)\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\gamma }_{i},{\\alpha }_{i},$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real numbers with $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following boundary conditions, defined on $x=a=1$ and $x=b=6.$\n\n$$\ny(1)=y(6),\\,{y}^{′}(1)={y}^{′}(6)\n$$\n\nThese are periodic boundary conditions." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363918 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363919 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363918 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363920 13194139533445 1759218