 \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+120 & =0 \\\\ 2(5)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+120 & =0 \\\\ 10\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-120 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{-120}{10} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364035 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364036 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364035 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364035 1077 "field-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364035 1079 #edb/ref 17592186364036 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364035 1080 #uuid "02ccb4c9-5daa-5666-8f75-86bfbbc4f6df" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364036 1081 "-12" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364036 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364036 1084 #uuid "0d6955c0-aea2-569c-9f9f-500aeecbad8d" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364037 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364038 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364037 1069 "q-345374" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364037 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364037 1071 "A body of mass $64\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}).$  At time $t>0$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t)=\\sqrt{t}$ meters.\n\nCalculate the magnitude of the force at time $t=4$ seconds. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe magnitude of the force equals\n{{field-1}} $\\text{N}.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364037 1072 #uuid "9ba5a4b9-764b-53b1-bfb9-bdc883b69f52" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364037 1074 "According to Newton’s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{-1/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{-1/2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{-3/2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4{(\\sqrt{t})}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the acceleration at $t=4\\,\\text{s}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(4) & =-\\frac{1}{4{(\\sqrt{4})}^{3}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4({2}^{3})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{32}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force in Newtons is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(4) & =ma(4) \\\\  & =64\\cdot -\\frac{1}{32} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the magnitude of the force is\n\n$$\n|F(4)|=|-2|=2\\,\\text{N}.\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364038 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364039 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364038 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364038 1077 "field-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364038 1079 #edb/ref 17592186364039 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364038 1080 #uuid "77b85336-61e4-5c1d-9e88-11a55fea4b1e" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364039 1081 "2" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364039 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364039 1084 #uuid "77e39a9e-abd6-5f6e-b019-f49fbb5fdab4" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364040 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364041 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364040 1069 "q-345383" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364040 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364040 1071 "A body of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=4{t}^{2}-32t+64$ meters per second.\n\nCalculate the time ${t}_{∗}$ at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude. Do not round the answer.\n\n${t}_{∗}=$\n\n{{field-1}}  $\\text{s}$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364040 1072 #uuid "588cda9c-3ac2-5ed9-a29e-46a4cf5ca1dc" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364040 1074 "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{2}-32t+64) \\\\  & =8t-32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8t-32 & =0 \\\\ 8t & =32 \\\\ t & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at ${t}_{∗}=4\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364041 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364042 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364041 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364041 1077 "field-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364041 1079 #edb/ref 17592186364042 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364041 1080 #uuid "4f0f029a-597f-5006-8cb2-9c7f0e31abcb" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364042 1081 "4" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364042 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364042 1084 #uuid "7e066ddd-42c6-5fe6-b40c-17a26331f73c" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364043 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364044 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364043 1069 "q-345392" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364043 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364043 1071 "A body of mass $4\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons. At time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t)=\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{4}-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}$ meters.\n\nCalculate the time ${t}_{∗}$ at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude. Do not round the answer.\n\n${t}_{∗}=$\n\n{{field-1}} $\\text{s}$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364043 1072 #uuid "f1a79434-99bb-53b8-8094-f70a16654e2f" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364043 1074 "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\,(\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{4}-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{3}-t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\,(\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{3}-t) \\\\  & =4{t}^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{t}^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ 4{t}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ t & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\ge 0,$ we disregard the negative solution. \n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at ${t}_{∗}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364044 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364045 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364044 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364044 1077 "field-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364044 1079 #edb/ref 17592186364045 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364044 1080 #uuid "ec03c53b-fea7-5fbd-9b3b-6edaf174ad7a" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364045 1081 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364045 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364045 1084 #uuid "403599fd-bf05-5e7f-a558-554e465cee45" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364046 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364047 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364046 1069 "q-345396" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364046 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364046 1071 "A body of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons. At time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t)=\\frac{1}{8}{e}^{-2t}(2{t}^{2}+8t-9)$ meters.\n\nCalculate the time ${t}_{∗}$ at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude. Do not round the answer.\n\n${t}_{∗}=$\n\n{{field-1}} $\\text{s}$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364046 1072 #uuid "b3458543-a878-501d-b389-3a5dcb32f5d4" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364046 1074 "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\,[\\frac{1}{8}{e}^{-2t}(2{t}^{2}+8t-9)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}[{e}^{-2t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}+8t-9)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-2t})\\cdot (2{t}^{2}+8t-9)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}[{e}^{-2t}(4t+8)-2{e}^{-2t}(2{t}^{2}+8t-9)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{e}^{-2t}[4t+8-2(2{t}^{2}+8t-9)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{e}^{-2t}(4t+8-4{t}^{2}-16t+18) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{e}^{-2t}(-4{t}^{2}-12t+26) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{e}^{-2t}\\cdot 2(13-6t-2{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-2t}(13-6t-2{t}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\,[\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-2t}(13-6t-2{t}^{2})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[{e}^{-2t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(13-6t-2{t}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-2t})\\cdot (13-6t-2{t}^{2})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[{e}^{-2t}(-6-4t)-2{e}^{-2t}(13-6t-2{t}^{2})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-2t}(-6-4t-26+12t+4{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-2t}(4{t}^{2}+8t-32) \\\\  & ={e}^{-2t}({t}^{2}+2t-8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2t}({t}^{2}+2t-8) & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}+2t-8 & =0 \\\\ (t+4)(t-2) & =0 \\\\ t & =-4,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\ge 0,$ we disregard the negative solution. \n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at ${t}_{∗}=2\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364047 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364048 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364047 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364047 1077 "field-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364047 1079 #edb/ref 17592186364048 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364047 1080 #uuid "9fb94801-0f86-51ab-8ea0-d007ab0a24bb" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364048 1081 "2" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364048 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364048 1084 #uuid "f9ed1c7c-9225-541b-a4e1-7e79d29d0697" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364049 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364050 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364049 1069 "q-346646" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364049 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364049 1071 "Find the area $A$ of the closed region between the curve $x=3y-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}$ and the $y$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364049 1072 #uuid "101266a1-932d-5412-9610-9dd0f5723079" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364049 1074 "First, we find the $y$-intercepts of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}y(6-y) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we draw a sketch of the region.\n\n![](images/4d/4d5f6214785ff51d2254d8e9a2f7b15e811df643c3a664ee46b56744ede46e53.png)\n\nWe want the area between the curve $x=3y-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}$ and the $y$-axis from $y=0$ to $y=6.$\nTo find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}(3y-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{6}y\\,\\text{d}y-\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{6}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{6}-\\frac{1}{6}{y}^{3}\\,{|}_{0}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(36-0)-\\frac{1}{6}(216-0) \\\\  & =54-36 \\\\  & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364050 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364051 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364050 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364050 1077 "field-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364050 1079 #edb/ref 17592186364051 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364050 1080 #uuid "dc6d31b9-d5cf-5448-af19-dffb931a4318" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364051 1081 "18" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364051 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364051 1084 #uuid "d19a552d-04e7-5215-ae91-03e1759285cd" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364052 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364053 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364052 1069 "q-346699" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364052 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364052 1071 "Find the area $A$ of the finite region bounded between the curve $x=\\frac{1}{9}{y}^{5}-\\frac{1}{9}{y}^{4}-\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3}$ and the $y$-axis for $y\\ge 0.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364052 1072 #uuid "4bf0e04f-545a-58e1-8ddb-f519ba88b9ee" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364052 1074 "First, we find the $y$-intercepts of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{9}{y}^{5}-\\frac{1}{9}{y}^{4}-\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}{y}^{3}({y}^{2}-y-6) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}{y}^{3}(y+2)(y-3) & =0 \\\\ y & =-2,\\,0,\\,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we draw a sketch of the region:\n\n![](images/8b/8bc9294352d263d7ff57eb257dae9a584b47927b18268ed0ddbe13ba0e2141ce.png)\n\nWe want to find the area between the curve $x=\\frac{1}{9}{y}^{5}-\\frac{1}{9}{y}^{4}-\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3}$ and the $y$-axis from  $y=0$ to $y=3.$ To find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(\\frac{1}{9}{y}^{5}-\\frac{1}{9}{y}^{4}-\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{y}^{5}\\,\\text{d}y-\\frac{1}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{y}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y-\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{6}}{6}\\,|}_{0}^{3}-\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{5}}{5}\\,|}_{0}^{3}-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}\\,|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}(\\frac{243}{2}-0)-\\frac{1}{9}(\\frac{243}{5}-0)-\\frac{2}{3}(\\frac{81}{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{2}-\\frac{27}{5}-\\frac{27}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{27}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtained a negative number because the region lies to the left of the $y$-axis. So, the area that we want is\n\n$$\nA=|-\\frac{27}{5}|=\\frac{27}{5}.\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364053 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364054 1319413