,\\text{s},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(1) & =⟨6(1)^{2},\\,\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{1}}+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6,\\,6+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the magnitude of $F(1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|F(1)| & =\\sqrt{(6)^{2}+{(6+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1))}^{2}} \\\\  & ≈10,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nSo, the magnitude of the resultant force is $10\\,\\text{N}.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364106 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364107 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364106 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364106 1077 "field-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364106 1079 #edb/ref 17592186364107 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364106 1080 #uuid "bc6904db-5188-57e1-aaef-5cbcdb73de7c" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364107 1081 "10" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364107 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364107 1084 #uuid "d3c2bc5d-95b2-5cca-89c2-19a0e28f9f08" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364108 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364109 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364108 1069 "q-352836" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364108 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364108 1071 "The displacement $r,$ in meters, of a particle $P$ of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the **magnitude** of the resultant force, in newtons, acting on the particle when $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$ Round your final answer **to the nearest integer**.\n\nThe magnitude of the resultant force is\n{{field-1}} $N.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364108 1072 #uuid "da6e145a-9626-57c3-8b94-732c48a87446" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364108 1074 "Newton’s second law states that\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\nwhere $F$ is the *resultant* force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo compute the acceleration vector, we first differentiate $r$ to get the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,\\frac{1}{t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate the velocity vector to get the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\,(\\frac{1}{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resultant force $F$ at time $t$ equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =2\\cdot ⟨-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $t=2\\,\\text{s},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2) & =⟨-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 2),\\,-\\frac{2}{(2)^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4),\\,-\\frac{1}{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the magnitude of $F(2)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|F(2)| & =\\sqrt{(-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4))^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & ≈6\\,\\text{N},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nSo, the magnitude of the resultant force is $6\\,\\text{N}.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364109 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364110 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364109 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364109 1077 "field-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364109 1079 #edb/ref 17592186364110 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364109 1080 #uuid "e4bbef26-3d01-5ba4-97b6-ffb8e079dcb7" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364110 1081 "6" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364110 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364110 1084 #uuid "0e7ea0a0-c89d-512e-b936-c0765689d390" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364111 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364112 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364111 1069 "q-352839" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364111 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364111 1071 "The displacement $r,$ in meters, of a particle $P$ of mass $3\\,\\text{kg}$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨2{e}^{t},\\,{t}^{4}⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the **magnitude** of the resultant force, in newtons, acting on the particle when $t=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{s}.$ Round your final answer **to the nearest integer**.\n\nThe magnitude of the resultant force is\n{{field-1}} $N.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364111 1072 #uuid "54af79b0-e8f7-5e06-a8d5-c5e9da34b194" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364111 1074 "Newton’s second law states that\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\nwhere $F$ is the *resultant* force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo compute the acceleration vector, we first differentiate $r$ to get the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{e}^{t},\\,4{t}^{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate the velocity vector to get the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{e}^{t},\\,12{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resultant force $F$ at time $t$ equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =3\\cdot ⟨2{e}^{t},\\,12{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6{e}^{t},\\,36{t}^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $t=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{s},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\,(\\frac{1}{2}) & =⟨6{e}^{1/2},\\,36{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6{e}^{1/2},\\,9⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the magnitude of $F\\,(\\frac{1}{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|F\\,(\\frac{1}{2})| & =\\sqrt{(6{e}^{1/2})^{2}+(9)^{2}} \\\\  & ≈13\\,\\text{N},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nSo, the magnitude of the resultant force is $13\\,\\text{N}.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364112 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364113 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364112 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364112 1077 "field-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364112 1079 #edb/ref 17592186364113 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364112 1080 #uuid "7a51a97f-26c7-5596-9d5e-395228af8a67" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364113 1081 "13" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364113 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364113 1084 #uuid "b6a3b4f2-9c66-52e9-af87-65239374249b" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364114 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364115 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364114 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364117 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364114 1069 "q-352842" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364114 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364114 1071 "A particle $P$ of mass $4\\,\\text{kg}$ is subject to the external forces \n\n$$\n{F}_{1}=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,8t⟩,\\,{F}_{2}=⟨4,\\,0⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere ${F}_{1}$ and ${F}_{2}$ are measured in newtons and $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the velocity of $P$ equals $⟨0,\\,1⟩\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s},$ calculate the velocity vector of $P$ at $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$v(2)=$\n$⟨\\,$\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\,⟩\\,m/s$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364114 1072 #uuid "3127c443-7863-5a24-80b0-92a974247e6f" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364114 1074 "Newton's second law states that\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\nwhere $F$ is the *resultant* force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nThe resultant force $F$ acting on $P$ equals the sum of the forces acting on $P.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & ={F}_{1}+{F}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,8t⟩+⟨4,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4,\\,8t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy Newton's second law, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{F}{m} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4,\\,8t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1,\\,2t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the velocity vector $v,$ we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\int a\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1,\\,2t⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1)\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int (2t)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t,\\,{t}^{2}⟩+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a (vector) constant of integration. \n\nWe can solve for $C$ by substituting $v=⟨0,\\,1⟩\\,\\text{m/s}$ and $t=0\\,\\text{s}$ into the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨0,\\,1⟩ & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+0,\\,{0}^{2}⟩+C \\\\ ⟨0,\\,1⟩ & =⟨0,\\,0⟩+C \\\\ C & =⟨0,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity of $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t,\\,{t}^{2}⟩+⟨0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t,\\,{t}^{2}+1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity at $t=2$ equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2)+2,\\,{2}^{2}+1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2)+2,\\,5⟩\\,m/s.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364115 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364116 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364115 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364115 1077 "field-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364115 1079 #edb/ref 17592186364116 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364115 1080 #uuid "c99b317a-851f-50bf-a9be-eca0fa78a9f1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364116 1081 "\\operatorname{sin}(2)+2" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364116 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364116 1084 #uuid "4f08e6e0-0850-5477-ad78-8773a5468ef9" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364117 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364118 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364117 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364117 1077 "field-2" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364117 1079 #edb/ref 17592186364118 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364117 1080 #uuid "47a7d0fb-5e67-5d20-9b5b-1db52eccf1d5" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364118 1081 "5" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364118 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364118 1084 #uuid "03d95a3d-1141-5f6e-b3eb-b3abc658edf2" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364119 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364120 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364119 1069 "q-352871" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364119 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364119 1071 "Compute the arc length $L$ of $y=\\frac{1}{3}{({x}^{2}-2)}^{3/2}$ for $x\\in [2,5].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$ \n{{field-1}}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364119 1072 #uuid "8a01c64a-af3a-516f-87ca-fa55cfb071bc" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364119 1074 "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{1}{3}{({x}^{2}-2)}^{3/2},$ $a=2,$ and $b=5.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{({x}^{2}-2)}^{1/2}\\cdot 2x=x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{[x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-2}]}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}({x}^{2}-2)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}-1)^{2}} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}={x}^{2}-1$ to the formula for the arc length, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}({x}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-x)\\,{|}_{2}^{5} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}(5)^{3}-5]-[\\frac{1}{3}(2)^{3}-2] \\\\  & =\\frac{110}{3}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364120 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364121 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364120 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364120 1077 "field-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364120 1079 #edb/ref 17592186364121 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364120 1080 #uuid "751a0142-744a-56a0-9299-d3c6e7357038" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364121 1081 "36" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364121 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364121 1084 #uuid "67f6be88-9065-59ca-9f9a-b9132ced30a1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364122 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364123 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364122 1069 "q-352951" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364122 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364122 1071 "A particle $P$ of mass $3\\,\\text{kg}$ is subject to the external forces \n\n$$\n{F}_{1}=⟨6{e}^{t},\\,\\frac{9}{t+2}⟩,\\,{F}_{2}=⟨3-6{e}^{t},\\,1-\\frac{9}{t+2}⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere ${F}_{1}$ and ${F}_{2}$ are measured in newtons and $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the velocity of $P$ equals $⟨-2,\\,0⟩\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s},$ calculate the speed of $P$ when $t=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{s}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe speed of $P$ when $t=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{s}$ is\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364122 1072 #uuid "d1a822cf-7228-5e87-b272-ee49925b9a48" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364122 1074 "Newton's second law states that\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\nwhere $F$ is the *resultant* force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nThe resultant force $F$ acting on $P$ equals the sum of the forces acting on $P.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & ={F}_{1}+{F}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨6{e}^{t},\\,\\frac{9}{t+2}⟩+⟨3-6{e}^{t},\\,1-\\frac{9}{t+2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy Newton's second law, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{F}{m} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ⟨3,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,\\frac{1}{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the velocity vector $v,$ we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\int a\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨1,\\,\\frac{1}{3}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 1\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t,\\,\\frac{1}{3}t⟩+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a (vector) constant of integration. \n\nWe can solve for $C$ by substituting $v=⟨-2,\\,0⟩\\,\\text{m/s}$ and $t=0\\,\\text{s}$ into the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨-2,\\,0⟩ & =⟨0,\\,\\frac{1}{3}(0)⟩+C \\\\ ⟨-2,\\,0⟩ & =⟨0,\\,0⟩+C \\\\ C & =⟨-2,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity of $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =⟨t,\\,\\frac{1}{3}t⟩+⟨-2,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t-2,\\,\\frac{1}{3}t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $t=\\frac{1}{2},$ the velocity equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\,(\\frac{1}{2}) & =⟨\\frac{1}{2}-2,\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{3}{2},\\,\\frac{1}{6}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the speed of $P$ when $t=\\frac{1}{2}$ equals the magnitude of $v\\,(\\frac{1}{2}).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|v\\,(\\frac{1}{2})| & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{82}}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the speed of $P$ when $t=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{s}$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{82}}{6}\\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364123 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364124 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364123 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364123 1077 "field-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364123 1079 #edb/ref 17592186364124 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364123 1080 #uuid "33f01783-60f1-5df9-b0c2-1427a11572a7" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364124 1081 "\\frac{\\sqrt{82}}{6}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364124 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364124 1084 #uuid "9f1580fc-6f47-5251-909b-d13dfe76819d" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364125 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364126 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364125 1069 "q-352952" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364125 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364125 1071 "A particle $P$ of mass $4\\,\\text{kg}$ is subject to the external forces \n\n$$\n{F}_{1}=⟨8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)⟩,\\,{F}_{2}=⟨12-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,10-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere ${F}_{1}$ and ${F}_{2}$ are measured in newtons and $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the velocity of $P$ equals $⟨1,\\,-1⟩\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s},$ calculate the speed of $P$ when $t=3\\,\\text{s}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe speed of $P$ when $t=3\\,\\text{s}$ is\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364125 1072 #uuid "2af21666-3fb9-5e01-b201-8023c19f529a" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364125 1074 "Newton's second law states that\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\nwhere $F$ is the *resultant* force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nThe resultant force $F$ acting on $P$ equals the sum of the forces acting on $P.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & ={F}_{1}+{F}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)⟩+⟨12-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,10-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨12,\\,10⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy Newton's second law, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{F}{m} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot ⟨12,\\,10⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,\\,\\frac{5}{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the velocity vector $v,$ we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\int a\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨3,\\,\\frac{5}{2}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 3\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int \\frac{5}{2}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3t,\\,\\frac{5}{2}t⟩+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a (vector) constant of integration. \n\nWe can solve for $C$ by substituting $v=⟨1,\\,-1⟩\\,\\text{m/s}$ and $t=0\\,\\text{s}$ into the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨1,\\,-1⟩ & =⟨3(0),\\,\\frac{5}{2}(0)⟩+C \\\\ ⟨1,\\,-1⟩ & =⟨0,\\,0⟩+C \\\\ C & =⟨1,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity of $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =⟨3t,\\,\\frac{5}{2}t⟩+⟨1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3t+1,\\,\\frac{5}{2}t-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $t=3,$ the velocity equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(3) & =⟨3(3)+1,\\,\\frac{5}{2}(3)-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨10,\\,\\frac{13}{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the speed of $P$ when $t=3$ equals the magnitude of $v(3).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|v(3)| & =\\sqrt{{10}^{2}+{(\\frac{13}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{569}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the speed of $P$ when $t=3\\,\\text{s}$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{569}}{2}\\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364126 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364127 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364126 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364126 1077 "field-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364126 1079 #edb/ref 17592186364127 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364126 1080 #uuid "bf5906c1-cb30-5e85-8951-2f1dfc45089e