\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩$ when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$, calculate the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364236 1072 #uuid "4dddd52f-17a1-50a5-b235-2c57e95a190a" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364236 1074 "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{C}_{1},-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩.$ \nSubstituting $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+{C}_{1},-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩ & =⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+{C}_{1},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+{C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{1} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+{C}_{2} \\\\ {C}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\nv=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩.\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364237 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364238 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364237 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364239 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364237 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364240 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364237 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364241 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364237 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364242 1319413953344