5 17592186045585 true] [17592186364553 1072 #uuid "008135f6-6225-50a5-8c82-16fdb8564c98" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364553 1074 "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $g(x,y,z)$ with three independent variables $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and $z=z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂r} & =\\frac{∂g}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂r}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂r}+\\frac{∂g}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂r} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂g}{∂x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $g$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}{z}^{4},\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}=3{y}^{2}{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =3{r}^{2}{z}^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =3{r}^{2}{z}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂r} & =(3{r}^{2}{z}^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +(3{r}^{2}{z}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3{r}^{2}{z}^{2}({z}^{2}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364554 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364555 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364554 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364556 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364554 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364557 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364554 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364558 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364554 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364559 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364554 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364554 1077 "selection" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364554 1079 #edb/ref 17592186364559 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364554 1080 #uuid "20825cbb-d704-5a1b-b17b-e68c8b914c7c" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364555 1081 "3{r}^{2}({z}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364555 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364555 1084 #uuid "d7772675-d258-5d51-b29d-b3396a5e5001" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364556 1081 "3{r}^{2}{z}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta )" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364556 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364556 1084 #uuid "2c7da375-0431-5f66-8543-0491e4f293c9" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364557 1081 "{z}^{2}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta " 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364557 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364557 1084 #uuid "43eedeea-a30d-5b9a-ba7b-6945237b5a90" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364558 1081 "{r}^{2}z({z}^{3}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta +z{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta )" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364558 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364558 1084 #uuid "b844a28f-f811-5210-9c5f-949a6a5243b2" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364559 1081 "3{r}^{2}{z}^{2}({z}^{2}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta )" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364559 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364559 1084 #uuid "eab23144-3264-5367-8737-fc9bde1fca89" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364560 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364561 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364560 1069 "q-95291" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364560 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364560 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- Any matrix of the form  $A=[\\begin{aligned}-a & 0 \\\\ 0 & a\\end{aligned}]$ has one negative singular value.\n- Any $5\\times 2$ matrix $A$ has at most $5$ distinct singular values.\n- Any $4\\times 5$ matrix $A$ has at most $5$ distinct singular values." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364560 1072 #uuid "4e856448-81c6-5a27-b63c-c2864d6eca93" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364560 1074 "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false.  All singular values of any matrix are non-negative. So, \n$A$  can't have any negative singular value.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $A$ is a $5\\times 2$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $2\\times 5$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $2\\times 2$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{2\\times 5}\\times {A}_{5\\times 2}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{2\\times 2}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $2$ distinct singular values.\n\n- Statement III is true.  Indeed,  since $A$ is a $4\\times 5$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $5\\times 4$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $5\\times 5$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{5\\times 4}\\times {A}_{4\\times 5}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{5\\times 5}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $5$ distinct singular values.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364561 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364562 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364561 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364563 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364561 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364564 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364561 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364565 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364561 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364566 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364561 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364561 1077 "selection" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364561 1079 #edb/ref 17592186364564 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364561 1080 #uuid "6075a19f-6e44