17592186045585 true] [17592186364608 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364608 1084 #uuid "ea2962b9-0d8d-5c0e-8d92-39f9f5aa6230" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364609 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364610 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364609 1069 "q-98642" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364609 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364609 1071 "![](images/cf/cff9a8967582489286a62b78234e147684d1e453c20967fe1190cf515afe246f.png)\n\nConsider the phase line shown above. Which of the following differential equations could have this phase line?\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}(y+3)$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y(y+3)^{2}$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}+3y$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364609 1072 #uuid "5a7147e0-2667-5230-a581-5d8792b92824" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364609 1074 "First, notice that all three differential equations are of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThe phase line gives us the following information:\n\n- The equilibrium solutions are $y=-3$ and $y=0.$\n\n- $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-3,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$\n\n- $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-3).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's study each of the given equations.\n\n- We start by considering ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}(y+3).$\n\nFirst, we see that $f(y)={y}^{2}(y+3),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-3,0.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nNow, we can construct a sign table for $f(y)$ as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-3)$ | $(-3,0)$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+3$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| ${y}^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is negative for  $y\\in (-\\infty ,-3),$ and $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-3,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$ This corresponds to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation I has the given phase line.\n\n- Next, we consider ${y}^{′}=y(y+3)^{2}.$\n\nFirst, $f(y)=y(y+3)^{2},$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-3,0.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)<0$ for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (-3,0).$ This does *not* correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation II does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Finally, we consider ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}+3y.$\n\nFirst, $f(y)=y(y+3),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-3,0.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)<0$ for $y\\in (-3,0).$ This does *not* correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation III does not have the given phase line.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364610 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364611 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364610 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364612 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364610 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364613 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364610 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364614 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364610 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364615 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364610 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364610 1077 "selection" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364610 1079 #edb/ref 17592186364612 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364610 1080 #uuid "e86b64bc-7b59-5c75-87d1-36e710ec3802" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364611 1081 "None of the listed" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364611 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364611 1084 #uuid "aa11f230-ac01-54c6-a241-9eb830650515" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364612 1081 "I only" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364612 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [1759218636