tions.\n\n- We start by considering ${y}^{′}=(y-2)(y-4)^{2}.$\n\nFirst, $f(y)=(y-2)(y-4)^{2},$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=2,4.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)>0$ for $y\\in (2,4)\\cup (4,\\infty ).$ This does *not* correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation I does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Next, we consider ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}-6y+8.$\n\nFirst, we see that $f(y)=(y-2)(y-4),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=2,4.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nNow, we can construct a sign table for $f(y)$ as follows: \n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,2)$ | $(2,4)$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y-2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-4$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for  $y\\in (-\\infty ,2)\\cup (4,\\infty ),$ and $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (2,4).$ This corresponds to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation II has the given phase line.\n\n- Finally, we consider ${y}^{′}=-{y}^{2}+6y-8.$\n\nFirst, $f(y)=-(y-2)(y-4),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=2,4.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)>0$ for $y\\in (2,4).$ This does *not* correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation III does not have the given phase line.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364617 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364618 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364617 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364619 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364617 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364620 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364617 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364621 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364617 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364622 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364617 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364617 1077 "